static.solution:
T(x) = f(a) + \frac{f'(a)}{1!} (x-a) + \frac{f''(a)}{2!} (x-a)^2 + ... + \frac{f^n(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n



f'(x) = e^{x}
f''(x) = e^{x}



So, in this case, 



T_2(x) = e^{0.8}+e^{0.8}\mathopen{}\left(x-0.8\right)+\frac{e^{0.8}}{2}\mathopen{}\left(x-0.8\right)^{2}



|e^{x} - T_2(x)| = \left|e^{0.8}+e^{0.8}\mathopen{}\left(-1.5\right)+\frac{e^{0.8}}{2}\cdot 2.25-e^{-0.7}\right| = 0.894378

statement.p:
Note that when the constant in the denominator is not 1, we still can use the geometric series, but we have to multiply by
a form of 1 that helps us.  We'll use \frac{\frac{1}{7}}{\frac{1}{7}}.
\frac{1}{(7+x)}=\frac{\frac{1}{7}}{\frac{1}{7}(7+x)}=\frac{\frac{1}{7}}{1+\frac{1}{7}x}=\frac{1}{7}\cdot \frac{1}{1+\frac{1}{7}x}

static.solution:
\displaystyle c_n = \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}, e.g. 



\displaystyle c_0 = \frac{f(0)}{0!} = 0. 



\displaystyle c_1 = \frac{f'(0)}{1!}; 



\displaystyle f'(x)=2 \arctan(8 x) + 2 x\frac{1}{(8 x)^2+1}8 
= 2 \arctan(8 x) + \frac{16 x}{64 x^2+1}; 



c_1 = 0.



\displaystyle c_2 = \frac{f''(0)}{2!}; 



\displaystyle 
f''(x) = \left( 2 \arctan(8 x) + \frac{16 x}{64 x^2+1} \right)' 



\displaystyle = 
\frac{16}{64 x^2+1} + \frac{16(64 x^2+1)-16 x64\cdot 2x}{(64 x^2+1)^2}



\displaystyle
= \frac{1024 x^2 + 16}{(64 x^2+1)^2} + \frac{16 - 1024 x^2}{(64 x^2+1)^2}
= \frac{32}{(64 x^2+1)^2};



\displaystyle c_2 = \frac{32}{2} = 16;



and so on. Another way is to use the power series for arctan:  



\displaystyle \arctan(t) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (-1)^n \frac{t^{2n+1}}{2n+1} 
= t-\frac{t^3}{3}+\frac{t^5}{5}-\frac{t^7}{7}+....



Then 



\displaystyle 2 x \arctan(8 x) = 
2 x \sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{(8 x)^{2n+1}}{2n+1} = 
\sum_{n=0}^\infty (-1)^n \frac{2\cdot 8^{2n+1}}{2n+1} x^{2n+2}
\displaystyle  
= 16 x^2 - \frac{1024}{3} x^4 + \frac{65536}{5} x^6 - \frac{4194304}{7} x^7+.... 



So c_0=0, c_1=0, c_2=16, c_3=0, c_4=-\frac{1024}{3}=-341.333333333333...



This is an alternating series. It converges if and only if 
\displaystyle \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{2 \cdot (8 x)^{2n+1}}{2n+1}=0. 
This limit is equal to 0 if and only if |8 x| \le 1, 
so \displaystyle |x|\le \frac{1}{8}. 
Therefore the radius of convergence is \displaystyle \frac{1}{8}.
